CHEATSHEET = """
# Sheep & Lamb Pain Recognition & Grimace Scale Manual (Concise Summary)

This is a condensed manual for assessing pain and affective states in sheep (*Ovis aries*) and lambs, synthesizing validated scales, Facial Action Units (FAUs), and behavioral indicators. 

---

## 1. Validated Assessment Scales
*   **SPFES (Adults):** Evaluates naturally occurring pain (e.g., footrot/mastitis) [@mclennan-2019; q="Development of a facial expression scale using footrot and mastitis as models of pain in sheep"].
*   **SGS (Adults):** Evaluates post-operative pain; uniquely includes the "Flehming" response [@hager-2017; q="The Sheep Grimace Scale as an indicator of post-operative distress"].
*   **LGS (Lambs):** Evaluates acute husbandry pain like tail docking [@guesgen-2016; q="Coding and quantification of a facial expression for pain in lambs"].
*   **USAPS (Behavioral):** The gold standard composite behavioral scale with the highest COSMIN validation evidence [@tomacheuski-2023; q="The UCAPS, UPAPS and USAPS showed the highest overall strength of evidence"].

## 2. Facial Action Units (FAUs) & Pain Signals
Scored typically on a 0 (Absent) to 2 (Obvious) scale.
*   **Eyes:** Pain presents as squinting or closure. Adults show half to full closure [@hager-2017; q="half-closed eyes were defined as an expression of “moderate” pain (score 1), whereas completely closed eyes were assigned with “severe” pain (score 2)"]; lambs show obvious squeezing [@guesgen-2016; q="If the eye closure reduces the visibility of the eye by more than half, it would be scored as obvious (2)"]. Automated systems flag "eyes partly closed" [@mota-rojas-2025; q="ears flipped and pinna not visible, with a “V” shaped nose, and eyes partly closed"].
*   **Ears:** In adults, ears flatten ventrally/caudally [@hager-2017; q="flattened ears and a slanted head were defined as an expression of moderate pain"]. In lambs, they point strictly backwards [@mclennan-2019; q="The ears of lambs point backwards when in pain, whereas in adult sheep the ears rotate ventrally and caudally"].
*   **Muzzle & Cheeks:** Adults exhibit prominent masseter muscle tension [@mclennan-2019; q="whereas in adult sheep the masseter muscle becomes more prominent"], while lambs show cheek flattening [@mclennan-2019; q="cheek area in lambs being flattened, whereas in adult sheep the masseter muscle becomes more prominent"]. Lambs' noses tighten into a 'V' shape [@guesgen-2016; q="pointing makes the nose appear more ‘V’ rather than ‘U’ shaped in frontal headshots"] and lips lose their natural upward curl [@guesgen-2016; q="There is lack of the ‘upwards curl’ at the edge of the lips that gives lambs their ‘smiling’ appearance"]. Severe pain in adults elicits the Flehming response [@hager-2017; q="Flehming represents a sign of severe pain"].
*   **Head Posture:** Drops below the withers [@silva-2025; q="Head below the withers. 2"] or hangs entirely [@hager-2017; q="hanging ears and head were defined as severe (score 2)"].

## 3. Body Behaviors & Holistic States
*   **Nociceptive Pain (USAPS):** Yields high diagnostic accuracy (AUC ~0.91) [@silva-2025; q="The area under the curve of 0.91 demonstrates the high discriminatory capacity of the scale"]. Key signs include an arched back and abnormal tail movement [@silva-2025; q="The most frequent postures after surgery were ‘arched back’ and ‘moves tail’"], alongside apathy and restlessness. Appetite is unreliable for acute pain scoring [@silva-2025; q="The item appetite can be excluded without affecting the USAPS cut-off point"].
*   **Fear/Stress:** Indicated by fleeing, agonism [@nc3rs-ethogram; q="Threat/thrust (rapid head and fore-body movements towards another animal); upright or sideways posture"], or freezing [@nc3rs-ethogram; q="Animal remains motionless when disturbed. Body is stiff (non-relaxed), and animal highly alert"].
*   **Positive Welfare:** Indicated by active play [@nc3rs-ethogram; q="Play behaviour contains the same behavioural elements as agonism, but is less intense, does not include injurious biting"] and allogrooming [@nc3rs-ethogram; q="Gentle grooming (sometimes reciprocal) of any part of another individual. Engaging in allogrooming is voluntary"].
*   **Neutral Baseline:** Relaxed immobility [@nc3rs-ethogram; q="Alert (eyes open) but with no directed attention, while sitting, standing, or leaning... Body relaxed and immobile."].

## 4. Confounders & Edge-Cases
*   **Restraint/Camera Angle:** Physical restraint alters natural FAUs, and 2D cameras struggle with depth perception [@guesgen-2016; q="restraint had some effect on facial expression and influenced both qualitative and quantitative measures"].
*   **False Positives:** Sedation, sleep, and aggression mimic pain grimaces [@cohen-2020; q="False positives are known to occur in a small range of scenarios such as sedation/anaesthesia, sleeping status, or during bouts of aggression"], and sedation alters behavior similarly to pain [@silva-2025; q="Behavior changes caused by sedation and pain may be similar, therefore sedation may be a confounding factor"].
*   **Morphology:** Heavy fleece or dark hair obscures visual FAUs [@mclennan-2019; q="difficulties in assessing the facial expression of longer-haired, muscular, darker-haired animals"].
*   **Coping Styles:** Some sheep hide pain via passive immobility, requiring sustained observation [@guesgen-2016; q="displaying a reactive coping style to pain, which is characterized by immobility and passivity"].
"""